WEBVTT

00:00:00,880 --> 00:00:17,020
Question about circulated tumor cells. I've been told they continue to be in your body for the duration of your life. It's just a matter of monetary.

00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:28,020
Are they inactive cells? Active cells stem cells? I know that RGCC looks at them, but do cancer marker tests also track them?

00:00:28,020 --> 00:00:40,020
Cancer marker tests do not track circulated tumor cells. Cancer markers themselves are different enzymes that are given off by a lot of different cells.

00:00:40,020 --> 00:00:48,020
Not just cancer cells, so that's why there's a normal range of tumor markers.

00:00:48,020 --> 00:01:02,020
So the normal range of a CA-153 is 0 to 35, meaning you could not have cancer and you could have a CA-153 of 15.

00:01:02,020 --> 00:01:09,020
Well, why would that be? Because they're not specific to cancer. Other cells give those off too.

00:01:10,020 --> 00:01:17,020
Circulated tumor cells are cancer stem cells, so they're not yet in a rapid replication phase.

00:01:17,020 --> 00:01:24,020
It is disputable that you have circulated tumor cells the rest of your life.

00:01:24,020 --> 00:01:44,020
I would argue that the chance of a person who had a diagnosis of cancer, having circulated tumor cells that are still alive but not maybe not in rapid replication, somewhere in the body is quite possible and highly probable.

00:01:44,020 --> 00:02:13,020
It should be always doing things to keep your cancer at bay. I've heard too many stories of trying to throw along my 25 years of helping people with cancer and people thinking that they've cured their disease that they stop using the right for they stop doing all the stuff that got them to the point of, you know, good spot with their cancer and then the cancer quote unquote comes back with the vengeance.

00:02:13,020 --> 00:02:26,020
Well, it's because it never was totally gone. There was circulated tumor cells. That's why I disagree and you've heard we say this a hundred times with an oncologist saying you have no evidence of disease.

00:02:26,020 --> 00:02:36,020
Well, that's actually a better terminology, which is a new term versus, you know, saying that you're cured.

00:02:36,020 --> 00:02:47,020
You don't have cancer anymore or something like that. You should always say that you're in remission. You're doing well, keep doing what you're doing to keep yourself there. That's my point of view.

